Pages = {381-388},
Month = {DEC 1},
Note = {22nd Annual Meeting of the Society for the Study of Ingestive Behavior,
   Seattle, WA, JUL 29-AUG 02, 2014},
Abstract = {Evidence linking sugar-sweetened beverage (SSB) consumption to weight
   gain and other negative health outcomes has prompted many individuals to
   resort to artificial, non-nutritive sweetener (NNS) substitutes as a
   means of reducing SSB intake. However, there is a great deal of
   controversy regarding the biological consequences of NNS use, with
   accumulating evidence suggesting that NNS consumption may influence
   feeding and metabolism via a variety of peripheral and central
   mechanisms. Here we argue that NNSs are not physiologically inert
   compounds and consider the potential biological mechanisms by which NNS
   consumption may impact energy balance and metabolic function, including
   actions on oral and extra-oral sweet taste receptors, and effects on
   metabolic hormone secretion, cognitive processes (e.g. reward learning,
   memory, and taste perception), and gut microbiota. (C) 2015 Elsevier
   Inc. All rights reserved.},
