Series = {SUSTAINABLE WORLD},
Year = {2003},
Volume = {6},
Pages = {337-346},
Note = {1st International Conference on Sustainable Planning and Development,
   Skiathos Isl, GREECE, OCT 01-03, 2003},
Organization = {Wessex Inst Technol; Univ Thessaly, Dept Planning \& Reg Dev, Sch Engn;
   Municipal Skiathos; Univ Thessaly, Res Comm; TEE Magnisia Reg Sect;
   Sovel; Themeliodomi SA; Coca Cola HBC},
Abstract = {This paper presents data on the social and economic value of the
   environment, applicable to urban planning, drawn from a literature
   review carried out by RSKENSR Environment Ltd. The research was
   commissioned by Greening for Growth, a partnership to deliver a
   long-term programme of environmental improvement and community action to
   regenerate the Northumberland Coalfield in the UK. Greening for Growth
   was established as a result of the publication of the Northumberland
   Coalfield Environmental Enhancement Strategy, which identifies a bold,
   comprehensive pound25 million programme, to be implemented over 20
   years. This study was commissioned to help make the business case to
   secure this investment. The study collated quantitative data on a range
   of benefits, including increased house prices, flood control and
   wastewater treatment, air-quality improvements, impacts on consumer
   behaviour and health and education benefits. Qualitative data was also
   collated, including benefits to quality of life, community capacity
   building, social inclusion, and impacts on safety and crime. Findings
   from the UK, USA and Europe collectively show that green space and trees
   offer significant economic and social value, much of which is not
   captured in the conventional planning process. For example, in SW
   England, the total economic value of woodland is around pound5,000 (is
   an element of7,100) per hectare, considering benefits due to forestry
   activities, tourism, carbon sequestration, biodiversity and landscape
   enhancement and community well being. Findings suggest that the Net
   Present Value of an urban tree in the UK is at least 200 (isan element
   of284), inferring that benefits far outweigh planting and maintenance
   costs. Overall, the study confirms that investing in green space and
   trees in urban areas is good value for money. The incorporation of such
   values into urban planning would raise the profile of the value of green
   space and delivermore sustainable planning schemes, enhancing the
   quality of life of urban residents as well as boosting the economy.},
