@article{ WOS:000238343400004,
Author = {Amollo, O},
Title = {Human rights and ethics in HIV and AIDS control programmes},
Journal = {DISCOVERY AND INNOVATION},
Year = {2005},
Volume = {17},
Number = {SI},
Pages = {7-20},
Month = {MAR},
Note = {1st NASAC Science Summit on HIV/AIDS Pandemic in Africa, Abuja, NIGERIA,
   SEP 20-23, 2004},
Organization = {Network African Sci Acad; Nigerian Acad Sci; African Acad Sci; Fed
   Minist Sci \& Technol},
Abstract = {The interplay between HIV and AIDS and Human Rights has not always been
   appreciated, and the latter sometimes thought to be a dispensable
   component in the fight against the spread of the HIV virus. People
   living with HIV and AIDS remain as ordinary, people and as people they,
   should never lose what is inherent to all of humanity human rights and
   human dignity. The fight against HIV and AIDS must be fought but not
   without regard to the guiding principles of human rights and human
   dignity catalysed only by stigma and unfounded fears. For, ultimately,
   the best forms of controls are those which recognize and respect the
   rights and dignity of people living with HIV/AIDS (PLWHAS), then engage
   them.
   Human rights have been regarded in the Universal Declaration of Human
   Rights as the equal and inalienable rights of all members of human
   community; ``an endowment by nature and nature's God to all men{''}; is
   the perspective adopted by the 1876 American Declaration of
   Independence.
   Ethics on the other hand, are the guiding principles and rules that
   dictate how any given conduct is undertaken and by whom, to what end the
   activity is to be executed.
   In the wake of advent of HIV and AIDS, humankind has reacted in the
   classical mode of reaction to every other epidemic, characterized by
   fear, stigma and discrimination of those plagued by any epidemic, in a
   misguided attempt to secure and protect the larger portion of the
   healthy society. The dawn of the HIV and AIDS epidemic witnessed mass
   testing and screening; quarantine and isolation; travel restrictions,
   and a host of other extraordinary measures aimed at curbing the malady.
   The issue of competing rights emerged, with a perceived competition
   between the need to uphold the rights of the individual on the one hand,
   and those of the larger public on the other hand. Often, individual
   rights have been sacrificed at the alter of the nebulous ``public
   interest{''}. Most of the responses have in truth been instructed by
   irrationality rather than genuine interest in public good. Communities,
   societies and nations jointly and severally forgot that it is in
   protecting the rights of the individual first, subject to limitations,
   that the public secures protection, ultimately achieving the public
   interest sought. The public, afterall, is nothing but a collectivity of
   individuals.
   Initial responses to the pandemic, were reactionary and actuated by fear
   and ignorance. In some countries, strong Berlin - like walls were
   erected to separate the healthy from the infected, and discriminatory
   treatment of HIV positive persons was common, justified as attempts to
   protect the greater society from contagion.
   In Kenya, the first HIV case was diagnosed in Nyeri in 1984, and the
   entire health personnel at the hospital abdicated. Other patients moved
   from the ward and nobody would serve the person with food. Being so
   driven from the hospital, he sought refuge in towns' hotels and nobody
   would accommodate him. He was driven from work, family, home and all
   that he knew. In reaction to the stigma, he moved to where no one knew
   him and, in vengeance to the cruelty and stigma, set out to suffer with
   others by infecting a number of unsuspecting sexual partners.
   In Hong Kong, the first case diagnosed was shrouded in secrecy, and a
   whole hospital floor was emptied to segregate the individual. Upon
   discharge, everything he had touched was sterilized and discarded(2).
   The international community has now developed much? and better
   understands the epidemic. Or does it really? Or have the breaches and
   flagrancies simply mutated from blatant disregard to subtle yet more
   punitive abuses of human rights of seropositive individuals? As recently
   as 1993, the US Government forcefully detained 300 HIV positive Haitians
   escaping after the 1991 coup d'Etat against erstwhile President Jean
   Betrand Aristide. They were forcefully detained at the Guantanamo Bay
   camp Buckely and in highly unsanitary wooden structures(3).},
Publisher = {ACADEMY SCIENCE PUBLISHERS},
Address = {PO BOX 14798, NAIROBI, KENYA},
