   THE CASE OF THE CITY HALL OF KATOWICE},
Booktitle = {PSYCHOLOGY AND PSYCHIATRY, SOCIOLOGY AND HEALTHCARE, EDUCATION, VOL II},
Series = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts},
Year = {2014},
Pages = {1025-1032},
Note = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {The article presents the manner in which people with disabilities of
   varying degree perceive the architectural barriers they encounter while
   using the services of the City Hall of Katowice. 23 variables connected
   with different types of architectural barriers were taken into account
   in the analysis; the perceived quality was graded on a 7-point scale. A
   further analysis was also carried out, exploring the relationship
   between the degree of a person's disability and the perceived quality of
   the facility. Disabilities were divided into 3 degrees: mild, moderate
   and severe. A non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis one-way analysis of variance
   was used to analyse the data. The studies conducted have shown that
   disabled persons perceive the way the City Hall has been adjusted to
   their needs as relatively good; they also point out certain areas which
   need improvement, such as parking lots, especially their number. The
   analysis of variance has indicated that people with various degrees of
   disability differed in their evaluation of the services of the City
   Hall. Severely disabled persons have rated the city hall's adaptation
   worse than those whose disabilities are moderate or mild.},
