   INTERDISCIPLINARY TASK, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {2002},
Pages = {625-630},
Note = {30th IAHS World Congress on Housing, UNIV COIMBRA, COIMBRA, PORTUGAL,
   SEP 09-13, 2002},
Organization = {Int Assoc Housing Sci},
Abstract = {The paper discusses the socio-economics characteristics of of new
   immigrants in a district of Istanbul. According to the survey conducted
   in that fringe area we observed four categories of residency sequences.
   These are: the newcoming settler; tenant in a squatter, owner of the lot
   and shelter, landlord of multiple squatters. Many characteristics of
   these groups such as: size of household and family, activities and
   social ties, age groups, migration origins, educational level,
   employment status, etc., are studied. The area displays many typical
   characteristics of squatter housing. Most of the settlers are from parts
   of the country where poverty is prevailing. Average age group is young.
   Occupational intensity is high. Family type and size reflect rural
   characteristics. One of the important outcomes of this study is that
   squatting is initiated by a necessity of shelter but after a period of
   time it is converted to an income generating and illegal urban activity.
   Residents are either satisfied are ambivalent to the quality of
   environment. They seem more interested in the value increment of their
   landholdings.},
