Abstract = {In everyday life, aside for food, clothing, and medicines, plants are
   also used for religious activities and are considered to have
   supranatural powers so that they are used as a complement of ritual
   ceremonies. Ethnobotany research of ``nyiwaq{''} ceremonies carried out
   by sasak people on Lombok Island was done by direct observation in the
   field in Kuta Village, Mandalika - Central Lombok and by interviewing
   ``Lalu{''} tuaadatand ``Papu{''} old parents of the Sasak community. At
   least 13 species were used for offerings. ``Nyiwaq{''} ceremony not only
   preserves the ritual ceremony with the unique offerings and
   complementary ceremonies presented but also shows the nature of
   cooperation among local people.},
