   Costelloe, JF
   CasasMulet, R
   Lyon, JP
   Stewardson, MJ},
Title = {TROPHIC VARIATION IN THE DARK CHUB, CANDIDIA TEMMINCKII, BASED ON
   CARBON AND NITROGEN STABLE ISOTOPE RATIOS IN THE TATARA RIVER SYSTEM,
   NORTHERN KYUSHU ISLAND, JAPAN},
Booktitle = {11TH INTERNATIONAL SYMPOSIUM ON ECOHYDRAULICS},
Year = {2016},
Note = {11th International Symposium on Ecohydraulics, Melbourne, AUSTRALIA, FEB
   07-12, 2016},
Abstract = {It is important for watershed management to understand and assess
   watershed condition correctly. The stable isotope ratio of organism is
   an important index for environment. In order to construct an effective
   stable isotope index, it is necessary to improve the understanding of
   its fundamental mechanisms in various environments of watershed. In
   order to collect fundamental knowledge about it, we investigated trophic
   variations in the dark chub, Candidia temminckii, based on carbon and
   nitrogen stable isotope ratios, and tried to reveal the relationship
   between these and landscape information of watershed. The dark chub is a
   small cyprinid fish found in western Japan and the Korean Peninsula that
   inhabits the middle and upper reaches of rivers. We studied 9 rivers in
   the Tatara River System, northern Kyushu, Japan. Dark chubs, periphytons
   and terrestrial leaf litters were collected from those rivers and
   analyzed for carbon and nitrogen stable isotope ratios. It was found
   that the contribution ratio of terrestrial leaf litter (CL) and trophic
   level (TL) of this species ranged widely and were positively correlated.
   Moreover, the best GLM explained the positive effects of a few
   variables, including forest area and site location, on these values,
   suggesting higher CL and TL in the upper streams flowing through a
   larger forest area.},
